--TEST--
Core\Signature::verify refuses an expired signature with a sentence of its own while every other way of not verifying keeps the one shared message, because expiry is checked after the tag — `rule:core-api/one-refusal-except-expiry`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound asserted on both sides: a lifetime still in the future verifies,
// one already past does not, and the refusal is the only one in this member
// that a caller can tell from the others. It is safe to distinguish precisely
// because it is reached *after* the tag has been checked — only the holder of
// a genuinely signed token ever sees it, and telling them the link has expired
// rather than that they are being attacked is why an application catches this.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
array<string> $claims = ["doc" => "7"];

Core\Time\Instant $hence = Core\Time::now()->plus(1h);
string $live = Core\Signature::sign($claims, {keys: $ring, until: $hence});
array<tainted string> $back = Core\Signature::verify($live, $ring);
echo $back["doc"] == "7" ? "a live signature verifies" : "refused a live one", "\n";

// One hour ago, so the token was already past its lifetime when it was minted
// — the state a signed URL reaches by sitting in a chat unfurl for a day.
Core\Time\Instant $ago = Core\Time::now()->minus(1h);
string $stale = Core\Signature::sign($claims, {keys: $ring, until: $ago});

// Both refusals are caught as the same class, so what distinguishes them is
// the sentence and not the type a program has to catch.
string $expired = "";
try {
    array<tainted string> $none = Core\Signature::verify($stale, $ring);
    echo "an expired signature verified", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $past) {
    $expired = $past->message;
}

string $forged = "";
try {
    array<tainted string> $nothing = Core\Signature::verify($live . "AAAA", $ring);
    echo "a tampered token verified", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $altered) {
    $forged = $altered->message;
}

// The message itself carries the two instants, so it is not printed here.
// What the case pins is that the two are *different* sentences, which is the
// whole of the rule, and that the shared one is unchanged.
echo $expired == $forged ? "one sentence for both" : "expiry has its own sentence", "\n";
echo $forged, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a live signature verifies
expiry has its own sentence
Core\Signature::verify(): $token is not a signature this ring made. Every way of not being one — text that is not base64, a token too short to hold a tag, a tag that authenticates under no key in $keys, a document this runtime did not write, and a token minted for another door — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
